WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.086 --> 00:00:08.116
FAYE MOSS: Hello and welcome back to Superstars in History , the talk show that features famous people from

2
00:00:08.117 --> 00:00:10.626
the past. I'm your host,

3
00:00:10.627 --> 00:00:17.466
Faye Moss. Today we're talking with a courageous woman who took a bus ride that helped change

4
00:00:17.467 --> 00:00:25.402
America. She didn't take discrimination sitting down, and her bravery inspired many others in the fight for equality.

5
00:00:25.403 --> 00:00:43.992
Please help me welcome Rosa Parks!

6
00:00:43.993 --> 00:00:47.571
ROSA PARKS: Thank you, Faye. It's nice to

7
00:00:47.572 --> 00:00:53.990
be here. FAYE MOSS: You're most famous for a very fateful bus ride, and I want to hear all

8
00:00:53.991 --> 00:00:58.759
about that day. But before that, why don't you tell us about the world you grew

9
00:00:58.760 --> 00:01:06.307
up in? ROSA PARKS: I was born in 1913 and grew up in and around Montgomery, Alabama, during the

10
00:01:06.308 --> 00:01:13.904
"Jim Crow" days. Jim Crow was a system of segregation laws and an unwritten code about how black people were to be treated in

11
00:01:13.905 --> 00:01:20.152
the South. Blacks went to different schools, ate in different restaurants, and drank from separate

12
00:01:20.153 --> 00:01:24.694
water fountains. We also had to sit in different parts of buses and

13
00:01:24.695 --> 00:01:28.904
trains. FAYE MOSS: Segregation laws were only part of

14
00:01:28.905 --> 00:01:36.070
the problem, right? ROSA PARKS: That's right. There were also terrorist groups like the Ku Klux Klan

15
00:01:36.071 --> 00:01:45.139
that targeted black people. By the time I was six, the Klan was riding through the black community, burning churches, beating

16
00:01:45.140 --> 00:01:48.485
people, and murdering. My grandfather told us to go to bed with our

17
00:01:48.486 --> 00:01:54.713
clothes on. That way if the Klan broke into our house, we would be ready to run and get

18
00:01:54.714 --> 00:01:57.672
out fast. FAYE MOSS: That must have

19
00:01:57.673 --> 00:02:02.705
been really scary. And back then it wasn't easy for black people to vote

20
00:02:02.706 --> 00:02:06.516
for better conditions. ROSA PARKS: The white people in charge made it difficult

21
00:02:06.517 --> 00:02:14.450
for blacks to vote. They made us take ridiculous tests—guess the number of jelly beans in a jar, things like that—before

22
00:02:14.451 --> 00:02:20.261
we could vote. They also charged a fee for voting, and most blacks were too poor

23
00:02:20.262 --> 00:02:26.212
to pay. Not being able to vote meant we couldn't elect leaders who would work to change

24
00:02:26.213 --> 00:02:28.420
the system. We had to change it

25
00:02:28.421 --> 00:02:32.697
ourselves. FAYE MOSS: Did you start by refusing to move to the back

26
00:02:32.698 --> 00:02:38.985
of the bus? ROSA PARKS: Well, that's what I was best known for—and it was

27
00:02:38.986 --> 00:02:41.167
a very important incident. But it

28
00:02:41.168 --> 00:02:48.985
wasn't how I started. By the 1950s I had been working for years with civil rights groups that were trying to make sure

29
00:02:48.986 --> 00:02:58.394
blacks could vote. I also helped raise money for the legal defense of people who were arrested for resisting Jim Crow laws—of course, I became one of

30
00:02:58.395 --> 00:03:01.841
those people! FAYE MOSS: Tell us more about that day

31
00:03:01.842 --> 00:03:06.342
on the bus. ROSA PARKS:

32
00:03:06.343 --> 00:03:11.783
It was December 1, 1955. At that point blacks had not demanded

33
00:03:11.784 --> 00:03:15.095
that buses be desegregated. We had just asked to be

34
00:03:15.096 --> 00:03:19.632
treated with more respect. I was on my way home from work as a seamstress for

35
00:03:19.633 --> 00:03:23.605
a department store. I took a seat just behind the "whites only" section

36
00:03:23.606 --> 00:03:31.059
of the bus. But when the bus got too full of white people, the bus driver told me to move further back so a white man could have my

37
00:03:31.060 --> 00:03:37.989
seat. I just sat there. He shouted at me and told me to move. I said,

38
00:03:37.990 --> 00:03:44.129
"No." Then he told me he would have me arrested. I said, "You can do that."

39
00:03:44.130 --> 00:03:47.199
FAYE MOSS: That was

40
00:03:47.200 --> 00:03:54.523
so brave! ROSA PARKS: Well, other black people had refused to give up their seats for whites before—I had

41
00:03:54.524 --> 00:03:58.327
done it before. But they usually just threw us

42
00:03:58.328 --> 00:04:07.532
off the bus. When this driver said he would have me arrested, I knew that if I let them take me to jail, we might be able to build a movement around

43
00:04:07.533 --> 00:04:16.476
this. And we did! The local black community, led by Dr. Martin Luther King Jr. organized a bus boycott.

44
00:04:16.477 --> 00:04:21.698
To protest my arrest, black people refused to ride on city buses.

45
00:04:21.699 --> 00:04:26.193
The leaders who organized the boycott intended it to only last one day, but

46
00:04:26.194 --> 00:04:31.431
the people of the community kept it up even after I was released from jail. FAYE MOSS:

47
00:04:31.432 --> 00:04:35.707
That's amazing how the community came together.

48
00:04:35.708 --> 00:04:41.880
ROSA PARKS: It wasn't easy. People had to get to work somehow, and few blacks at the time had

49
00:04:41.881 --> 00:04:47.855
cars. Black churches raised money to buy station wagons and formed car pools to take people to work.

50
00:04:47.856 --> 00:04:52.395
The white leaders of the city of Montgomery were not happy.

51
00:04:52.396 --> 00:04:57.071
The city buses were losing a lot of money because we weren't riding them. The

52
00:04:57.072 --> 00:05:05.047
police harassed the car pool drivers, giving them tickets for traffic violations even when they hadn't committed one. I got

53
00:05:05.048 --> 00:05:09.055
many death threats. Dr. King's home was bombed. Fortunately he

54
00:05:09.056 --> 00:05:11.806
and his family weren't harmed. But we

55
00:05:11.807 --> 00:05:22.740
carried on brave and strong. The boycott lasted for 381 days. FAYE MOSS: That's

56
00:05:22.741 --> 00:05:26.575
more than a year! How did it end? ROSA

57
00:05:26.576 --> 00:05:31.155
PARKS: Well, while we were all protesting and refusing to ride the buses,

58
00:05:31.156 --> 00:05:36.681
the movement's lawyers were working in the courts to challenge the segregation laws. Then

59
00:05:36.682 --> 00:05:49.797
on December 20, 1956, a Supreme Court ruling went into effect that made bus segregation illegal. We had won. FAYE MOSS:

60
00:05:49.798 --> 00:05:52.480
Was that the end of the movement?

61
00:05:52.481 --> 00:05:55.675
ROSA PARKS: Oh no! It was

62
00:05:55.676 --> 00:06:04.179
the beginning. The bus boycott showed black Americans that if we could stand up to unfairness and inequality, things would

63
00:06:04.180 --> 00:06:10.756
change. The civil rights movement continued to grow. We got many new laws passed to protect our rights.

64
00:06:10.757 --> 00:06:18.425
For example, the Civil Rights Act of 1964 made it illegal to discriminate against blacks.

65
00:06:18.426 --> 00:06:23.744
The Voting Rights Act of 1965 protected our right to vote. As

66
00:06:23.745 --> 00:06:28.578
for me and my husband, after the bus boycott we both lost our jobs. Eventually we

67
00:06:28.579 --> 00:06:33.580
moved north to Detroit, Michigan, to find work. FAYE MOSS:

68
00:06:33.581 --> 00:06:38.254
So did you give up activism and settle down to a peaceful life? ROSA

69
00:06:38.255 --> 00:06:43.753
PARKS: Not quite. I guess I was just born to be a

70
00:06:43.754 --> 00:06:48.737
troublemaker. I continued to work in the civil rights movement and stayed involved in politics.

71
00:06:48.738 --> 00:06:52.906
For many years I was an assistant to a U.S. Congress member. I

72
00:06:52.907 --> 00:06:57.729
kept working for good causes as long as I could! FAYE

73
00:06:57.730 --> 00:07:01.080
MOSS: You are truly an

74
00:07:01.081 --> 00:07:04.607
inspiration. Thank you so much for joining us today, Mrs. Parks.

75
00:07:04.608 --> 00:07:08.040
We'll let you get back to history now.

76
00:07:08.041 --> 00:07:13.632
ROSA PARKS: Thank

77
00:07:13.633 --> 00:07:21.850
you, Faye. FAYE MOSS: Rosa Parks spent most of her life fighting injustice and inspired a generation of activists to

78
00:07:21.851 --> 00:07:28.364
do the same. In 1996 President Bill Clinton awarded her the Presidential Medal

79
00:07:28.365 --> 00:07:36.631
of Freedom. She died in 2005 at the age of 92. Her body lay in state at the U.S.

80
00:07:36.632 --> 00:07:42.135
Capitol. She was the first woman and the second African American to be honored in this

81
00:07:42.136 --> 00:07:45.321
way. I hope you enjoyed today's interview.

82
00:07:45.322 --> 00:07:52.515
I'll be back soon with another famous face from history!

